1162. As Far from Land as Possible

- LeetCode: https://leetcode.com/problems/as-far-from-land-as-possible/
- Topic: Graphs, BFS, Multi-Source BFS, Matrix
- Language: Java
- Status: Solved ✅

1. My Intuition

After solving 01 Matrix (LeetCode 542), I recognized that this problem follows the same Multi-Source BFS pattern.

Instead of finding the nearest zero for every cell, here I need to find the water cell whose distance to the nearest land cell is maximum.

Therefore:

- All land cells ("1") are the sources.
- Water cells ("0") are the cells whose distances need to be calculated.
- Starting BFS from all land cells simultaneously allows me to calculate the shortest distance of every water cell to its nearest land cell.

After calculating the distances, I can maintain a variable "max" to store the maximum distance.

2. My Approach

1. Create a distance matrix "dist" of the same dimensions as the input grid.
2. Initialize a queue and "max = 0".
3. Iterate through the entire grid:
   - If the cell contains land ("1"), assign distance "0" and insert its coordinates into the queue.
   - Otherwise, initialize its distance as "-1", representing an unvisited water cell.
4. Define four direction arrays to explore up, down, left, and right.
5. Perform Multi-Source BFS:
   - Remove the current cell from the queue.
   - Explore its four neighbours.
   - Check whether the neighbour is within the grid boundaries and unvisited.
   - Calculate its distance using "dist[r][c] + 1".
   - Update "max" if the newly calculated distance is greater.
   - Insert the neighbour into the queue.
6. Continue until the queue becomes empty.
7. If "max == 0", return "-1", because either all cells are land or all cells are water.
8. Otherwise, return "max".

3. Where I Got Stuck / My Mistake

There was no major implementation mistake.

The main challenge was identifying that this problem could be solved using the same Multi-Source BFS approach as 01 Matrix.

Instead of performing BFS separately from every water cell, I used all land cells as simultaneous starting points.

4. What Clicked

- The same Multi-Source BFS template can solve different problems by changing the source condition and required output.
- In 01 Matrix, zero cells are the sources.
- In this problem, land cells are the sources.
- BFS calculates the shortest distance from each cell to its nearest source.
- Since the problem asks for the maximum of these shortest distances, I maintain a "max" variable.
- If the grid contains only land or only water, the answer is "-1".

Key concept: First calculate the shortest distance from every water cell to its nearest land cell using Multi-Source BFS, then find the maximum among those distances.

5. Final Submitted Code

class Solution {
    public int maxDistance(int[][] grid) {

        int m = grid.length;
        int n = grid[0].length;

        int max = 0;

        int[][] dist = new int[m][n];

        Queue<int[]> q = new LinkedList<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {

                if (grid[i][j] == 1) {
                    dist[i][j] = 0;
                    q.offer(new int[]{i, j});
                } else {
                    dist[i][j] = -1;
                }
            }
        }

        int[] dr = {-1, 1, 0, 0};
        int[] dc = {0, 0, -1, 1};

        while (!q.isEmpty()) {

            int[] curr = q.poll();

            int r = curr[0];
            int c = curr[1];

            for (int k = 0; k < 4; k++) {

                int nr = r + dr[k];
                int nc = c + dc[k];

                if (nr >= 0 && nr < m &&
                    nc >= 0 && nc < n &&
                    dist[nr][nc] == -1) {

                    dist[nr][nc] = dist[r][c] + 1;

                    if (dist[nr][nc] > max) {
                        max = dist[nr][nc];
                    }

                    q.offer(new int[]{nr, nc});
                }
            }
        }

        return max == 0 ? -1 : max;
    }
}

6. Complexity Analysis

Let "n" be the dimension of the square grid.

- Time Complexity: "O(n²)" — Every cell is inserted into and processed from the queue at most once. Each cell checks four neighbours.
- Space Complexity: "O(n²)" — Distance matrix and queue.

7. Revision Notes

- Identify all land cells as sources.
- Insert all sources into the queue with distance "0".
- Use Multi-Source BFS to calculate the nearest land distance for every water cell.
- Maintain a maximum distance while exploring.
- Return "-1" if there is no valid land-water combination.
- Recognize that the same BFS algorithm can solve different problems by changing the source condition and final objective.

Pattern learned: Multi-Source BFS — Find the shortest distance to the nearest source, then aggregate the distances according to the problem requirement.
